# Day 02 – Windows Security Event IDs & Log Analysis

## Overview
On Day 02 of my SOC Analyst training, I explored important Windows Security Event IDs and practiced analyzing logon events using Windows Event Viewer.

## Objectives
- Understand important Windows Security Event IDs.
- Identify successful and failed logon events.
- Analyze logon details.
- Understand different Windows Logon Types.
- Compare Event ID 4624 and 4625.

## Event IDs Studied

| Event ID | Description |
|---|---|
| 4624 | Successful Logon |
| 4625 | Failed Logon |
| 4634 | Account Logged Off |
| 4672 | Special Privileges Assigned |
| 4740 | Account Locked Out |

## Practical Lab

### 1. Successful Logon Analysis
- Opened Windows Event Viewer.
- Navigated to Windows Logs → Security.
- Filtered Event ID 4624.
- Inspected successful logon records.

### 2. Event Details Investigation
Analyzed the following fields:
- Account Name
- Logon Type
- Workstation Name
- Source Network Address
- Time Created

### 3. Event Comparison
Compared Event ID 4624 (Successful Logon) with Event ID 4625 (Failed Logon).

## Important Learning
**Logon Type 10 – Remote Interactive:** Commonly associated with Remote Desktop logons.

A successful logon does not automatically mean activity is safe. Similarly, a failed logon does not automatically indicate a brute-force attack. Security analysts should investigate timestamps, account names, source addresses, frequency, and related events.

